<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Dečanski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1285–<a href="/years/1331">1331</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Stefan Uroš III Dečanski</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Uroš III Dečanski was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1321 to 1331, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a>. He won a decisive victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-velbazhd">Battle of Velbazhd</a> in 1330, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-shishman">Michael Shishman</a> was killed. He founded the monastery of Visoki Dečani, from which his name comes, and was overthrown in 1331 by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> and died soon afterwards in prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-decanski.jpg" alt="Stefan Dečanski" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BLAGO Fund, Inc. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Uro%C5%A1_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
